{"@context":"http://iiif.io/api/presentation/3/context.json","id":"https://spokenweb.github.io/Ultimatumi1985/manifests/alan-lord-at-ultimatum-festival-1985-05-01.json","type":"Manifest","label":{"en":["Alan Lord at Ultimatum Festival 1985-05-01"]},"homepage":[{"id":"https://spokenweb.github.io/Ultimatumi1985","type":"Text","label":{"en":["Alan Lord at Ultimatum Festival 1985-05-01"]},"format":"text/html"}],"metadata":[{"label":{"en":["Description"]},"value":{"en":["The following is a transcription of Alan Lord’s performance at Ultimatum, Wednesday May 1st, 1985 at the Foufounes Électriques bar and performance space in Montréal, Québec, Canada. Alan Lord is the festival organizer and creator of Ultimatum. He is a Canadian musician, writer and engineer, and was a member of the bands Vex, the Marauders, Vent du Mont Scharr and Alan Lord & the Falling Men. An important figure for Montréal’s alternative punk scene, he is featured in Erik Cimon’s documentary “Mtl Punk -- The first wave” (2011). During his Ultimatum set, he performed four song-poems, including “Too Much Money,\" \"Everybody,\" and “Ignoring the Future Today.\" The song-poems were accompanied by computer graphics and videos created by Boris Wanowitch aka Pierre Zovilé using an Apple II computer, and electronic music tapes by Boys du Sévère (Jean-Luc Bonspiel and Jean-Martin Mignault). “Too Much Money” and “Everybody” were originally performed by Lord’s 1984 band Débris. Lord wrote the music and lyrics for each of the song-poems, except for “Ignoring the Future Today” which was composed by Jean-Luc Bonspiel. The Ultimatum LP features “Ignoring the Future Today.” "]}},{"label":{"en":["Language"]},"value":{"en":["English"]}}],"items":[{"id":"https://spokenweb.github.io/Ultimatumi1985/alan_lord_at_ultimatum_festival_1985_05_01/canvas/5","type":"Canvas","duration":1016,"annotations":[{"@context":"http://iiif.io/api/presentation/3/context.json","id":"https://spokenweb.github.io/Ultimatumi1985/manifests/alan_lord